course: dictionary
lesson: 10974
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Many words have more than one meaning, and ward is a good example. To find the right one, read the words around it, which is the context. «Intensive care is a special hospital ward where very poorly babies are watched day and night.» Hospital and babies being watched tell you a ward here is a room with beds.
explain: Try each meaning in the sentence and see which one fits. «On the morning ward round doctors review each patient and plan the day's treatment.» A ward round is a visit to the beds, so a hospital room fits. If your meaning makes the sentence silly, try another.
explain: Ward can also be a verb, and then it does a different job. «Washing hands helps to ward off colds» means it keeps colds away. When ward comes with a little word like off, read the whole phrase to find the meaning.
fact: A ward is a room in a hospital with beds for patients.
fact: «Children's wards» have nurses, doctors and play specialists, and usually a bed or chair so a parent can stay overnight.
fact: A ward can also be an area of a town or city that votes for its own councillor.
fact: A ward can be a child who is looked after by a guardian or court, not by their parents.
fact: «Ward off» is a verb phrase meaning to keep something bad away, such as germs.
exercise: «Intensive care is a special hospital ward where very poorly babies are watched.» What is a ward here? | A child looked after by a guardian | *A room in a hospital with beds for patients | An area of a town that votes for a councillor | To keep germs away
exercise: A rich family looks after a girl who is a ward of the family, not her parents. What does ward mean here? | An area that votes for its own councillor | *A child who is looked after by a guardian or court | A room in a hospital | To keep something bad away
exercise: «Washing hands helps to ward off colds.» What does ward off mean? | To visit a hospital room | *To keep something bad away | To vote for a councillor | To look after a child
test: The local ward chooses its own councillor. What is a ward in this sentence? | *An area of a town or city that votes for its own councillor | A room in a hospital with beds | To keep something bad away | A child looked after by a guardian
test: What does ward mean in «Children's wards have nurses, doctors and play specialists»? | *A room in a hospital with beds for patients | A child who is looked after by a guardian | An area that votes for a councillor | To keep germs away
exam: Which meaning of ward fits «On the morning ward round doctors review each patient»? | *A hospital room with beds for patients | A child looked after by a guardian | To keep something bad away | An area that votes for its own councillor
